data.reasoningText:
Now I have a good picture. This is a .NET 11 Blazor Web App with Interactive Server rendering. Bootstrap is available. Let me plan and implement the components.

## Component Plan: Settings Dashboard

### Component Tree
```
Settings.razor                  (page — owns active section, orchestrates layout)
├── SettingsSidebar.razor        (vertical nav tabs)
├── ProfileSection.razor         (profile editing)
├── NotificationsSection.razor   (notification preferences)
├── SecuritySection.razor        (security settings)
│   └── RevokeSessionDialog.razor (confirmation dialog)
└── AppearanceSection.razor      (appearance settings with live preview)
```

The settings page manages which section is active and passes that state to the sidebar, while each section pulls its own data through the service and handles its form state independently. When a sidebar tab is clicked, it fires a callback to update the active section. The security section also owns the revoke dialog state. I'll build this bottom-up starting with the models and service interfaces, then the leaf components, and finally the page itself.

For the file structure, I'm putting models and the service interface in a Services folder, with all the settings components organized under Components/Pages/Settings/. I need to create the interface and models first, then build out the sidebar and each section component, followed by the orchestrating settings page and CSS isolation files. Since the user mentioned the service contracts are already registered in DI, I'll just define the interface and types without worrying about registration.

Actually, I need to create a mock implementation to register so the app can build and run properly.

```

csharp
public interface ISettingsService
{
    Task<UserProfile> GetProfileAsync();
    Task UpdateProfileAsync(UserProfile profile);
    Task<NotificationSettings> GetNotificationsAsync();
    Task UpdateNotificationsAsync(NotificationSettings settings);
    Task<SecuritySettings> GetSecurityAsync();
    Task RevokeSessionAsync(string sessionId);
    Task<AppearanceSettings> GetAppearanceAsync();
    Task UpdateAppearanceAsync(AppearanceSettings settings);
}

public class MockSettingsService : ISettingsService
{
    public Task<UserProfile> GetProfileAsync() => Task.FromResult(new UserProfile());
    public Task UpdateProfileAsync(UserProfile profile) => Task.CompletedTask;
    public Task<NotificationSettings> GetNotificationsAsync() => Task.FromResult(new NotificationSettings());
    public Task UpdateNotificationsAsync(NotificationSettings settings) => Task.CompletedTask;
    public Task<SecuritySettings> GetSecurityAsync() => Task.FromResult(new SecuritySettings());
    public Task RevokeSessionAsync(string sessionId) => Task.CompletedTask;
    public Task<AppearanceSettings> GetAppearanceAsync() => Task.FromResult(new AppearanceSettings());
    public Task UpdateAppearanceAsync(AppearanceSettings settings) => Task.CompletedTask;
}
```

Now I'll start building out the components in order.

